<p class="text-lg text-silver-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
  A certificate of title is the state's ownership record. The brand printed on it is a separate fact from the car's condition, and a brand applied in one state is not always reprinted the same way after the car is retitled somewhere else. Read the brand, then check the VIN against the sources below before you treat a listing as a clean car.
</p>

<h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Brands you will actually see</h2>
<div class="space-y-4 mb-10 text-sm text-silver-400 leading-relaxed">
  <p><strong class="text-white">Clean.</strong> No brand is printed. That is not a promise that the car was never damaged, never in a flood, or never repurchased. It means this state's title record does not carry one of the brands below.</p>
  <p><strong class="text-white">Salvage.</strong> An insurer, and in some states the owner, has declared the car a total loss. The dollar threshold that triggers the brand is set by that state, so the same repair bill can brand a car in one state and not in another. The <a href="/guides/title/salvage-title-guide/" class="text-volt-400 hover:underline">salvage-title guide</a> covers what happens next, including insurance and resale.</p>
  <p><strong class="text-white">Rebuilt or reconstructed.</strong> A salvage car that later passed whatever inspection that state requires before it can be registered again. The brand usually stays on the title. Passing the inspection means the state allowed it back on the road. It does not restore the car to the value of a never-branded twin.</p>
  <p><strong class="text-white">Flood.</strong> A water-damage brand. States do not all use the same depth or duration rule, and a car that was wet can move without the word "flood" ever being printed. Corrosion in connectors, a musty cabin, and silt in the spare-tire well are the physical checks. The title is the record check.</p>
  <p><strong class="text-white">Lemon or manufacturer buyback.</strong> The manufacturer repurchased or replaced the car under a lemon law, and the state required a brand. The brand can be missed when the title is issued in a second state. Ask for the repurchase paperwork, not only the current title.</p>
  <p><strong class="text-white">Junk or nonrepairable.</strong> The state that applied the brand has decided the car should not be titled for road use. Parts cars are the usual result. A later title from another state that drops the word is a reason to stop, not a reason to treat the car as rebuilt.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Two records that are not the title</h2>
<div class="space-y-4 mb-10 text-sm text-silver-400 leading-relaxed">
  <p><strong class="text-white">NMVTIS.</strong> The National Motor Vehicle Title Information System, published for consumers at <a href="https://vehiclehistory.gov" class="text-volt-400 hover:underline" rel="noopener">vehiclehistory.gov</a>, collects title, junk, salvage, and insurance total-loss data from states, insurers, and salvage yards that are required to report. A report can show a brand or a total-loss record the current title omits. It does not list every repair, every accident, or the price you should pay.</p>
  <p><strong class="text-white">Odometer disclosure.</strong> A transfer of ownership generally includes a written mileage disclosure under 49 CFR Part 580. Section 580.17 exempts a vehicle that is 20 years old or older, so a 2006 model in 2026 can change hands without that federal disclosure. The exemption is about the paperwork, not about whether the odometer is accurate. Compare the number on the car with the title, the service records, and any prior disclosure you can find.</p>
  <p>State fees, inspection rules, and the office that actually issues the title are on each <a href="/states/" class="text-volt-400 hover:underline">state registration guide</a>. A lien release, the signed title, and the odometer disclosure when one is required are the usual packet. The fee is whatever that state lists, not a national amount.</p>
</div>

